/-!
# Normality ascends along a flat ring map with normal fibres

Proof of `isNormalRing_of_flat_of_isNormalRing_fiber` (Stacks 0C22): if `φ : R → S` is a flat map
of Noetherian rings, `R` is a normal ring and every fibre ring `κ(𝔭) ⊗_R S` is a normal ring, then
`S` is a normal ring.

The argument is the source's. For a Noetherian ring, being normal is the conjunction of Serre's
conditions `(R_1)` and `(S_2)` (Lemma 031S), so the hypotheses say that `R` and all the fibre
rings have `(R_1)` and `(S_2)`. Each of those two conditions ascends along a flat map of
Noetherian rings whose fibre rings satisfy it — `(S_2)` by Lemma 0339 and `(R_1)` by Lemma 033A —
so `S` has `(R_1)` and `(S_2)`, that is, `S` is normal by Lemma 031S again.

For the `(R_1)` half the argument is spelled out here. Fix a prime `𝔮` of `S` of height `≤ 1` and
let `𝔭 = φ^{-1}(𝔮)`. Flatness gives going down, so heights add along `𝔭 ⊆ 𝔮`, that is
`ht(𝔮) = ht(𝔭) + ht(𝔮/𝔭S)`, whence both summands are `≤ 1`. Thus `R_𝔭` is regular by `(R_1)` for
`R`, and the closed fibre `S_𝔮/𝔭S_𝔮`, which is the localization of the fibre ring `κ(𝔭) ⊗_R S` at
the prime corresponding to `𝔮`, is regular by `(R_1)` for that fibre ring. Regularity then ascends
along the flat local homomorphism `R_𝔭 → S_𝔮`: dimension is additive,
`dim S_𝔮 = dim R_𝔭 + dim S_𝔮/𝔭S_𝔮`, while the maximal ideal of `S_𝔮` is generated by generators of
`𝔭S_𝔮` together with lifts of generators of the maximal ideal of the closed fibre, so the
embedding dimension of `S_𝔮` is at most `dim R_𝔭 + dim S_𝔮/𝔭S_𝔮 = dim S_𝔮`.

## Main results

* `Stacks.Flatness.NormalFibreAscent.solution`: the statement of Stacks Tag 0C22.

## Implementation notes

Lemmas 031S and 0339 are used in the form the providers of Stacks Tags 031S and 0339 state them.
The library already carries Lemma 033A, as `property_Rk_of_flat_of_fibre_of_base` in
`StacksFrontier.RingTheory.Serre.FlatAscent`, but that module could not be imported alongside those
two: it reaches a second copy of `Module.depth` through `StacksFrontier.RingTheory.Serre.Defs` and
`StacksFrontier.RingTheory.Depth.Defs`, while those two use the copy of Stacks Tag 00LI, and the two
declarations have the same name. So the `(R_1)` ascent is proved here as
`isRegularLocalRing_atPrime_of_flat_of_fiber`, together with the two ring-theoretic facts it rests
on and which Mathlib does not have: generation subadditivity along a quotient
(`spanFinrank_le_add_spanFinrank_map_quotient`) and regularity ascent along a flat local
homomorphism with regular closed fibre (`isRegularLocalRing_of_flat_of_regular_closedFibre`).
Unlike the library's 033A this version is stated for `Ideal.Fiber`, the `κ(𝔭) ⊗_R S` that 031S and
0339 use, rather than for `S ⊗_R κ(𝔭)`, which removes a transport along
`Algebra.TensorProduct.comm`, and it takes the identification of the localization of the fibre
ring with the closed fibre from Mathlib's `Ideal.Fiber.algEquivAux₂` rather than rebuilding it.

No Noetherian hypothesis on the fibre ring is needed: `Ideal.Fiber.algEquivQuotient` presents it
as a quotient of a localization of `S`, so `isNoetherianRing_fiber` supplies the instance that
Serre's criterion needs at each fibre.

Normality is the library's `IsNormalRing` of tag 00GV, which is by definition the `IsDomain` plus
`IsIntegrallyClosed` condition on every `Localization.AtPrime` that 031S writes out;
`isNormalRing_iff_isRegularLocalRing_and_min_two_le_depth` records that, so 031S is applied to
`R`, to each fibre ring and to `S` through a single name.

